1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,041
Black holes are, by definition, dark.
Nothing that crosses the event horizon

2
00:00:05,041 --> 00:00:10,539
ever comes back out. And yet the regions
around black holes are among the brightest

3
00:00:10,539 --> 00:00:12,045
places in the universe.

4
00:00:12,367 --> 00:00:17,160
The light doesn't come from the black hole
itself. It comes from gas spiraling

5
00:00:17,160 --> 00:00:21,287
inward, in a flattened, glowing structure
called an accretion disk.

6
00:00:21,467 --> 00:00:26,891
Here is the puzzle we'll unpack. Disks
around small black holes, a few times the

7
00:00:26,891 --> 00:00:32,382
mass of the Sun, shine mostly in X-rays.
Disks around supermassive black holes, a

8
00:00:32,382 --> 00:00:37,738
hundred million times heavier, shine
mostly in ultraviolet light. Same physics,

9
00:00:37,738 --> 00:00:39,212
different colors. Why?

10
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:46,417
Let's build the simplest model that could
possibly work. Picture the disk as a thin,

11
00:00:46,417 --> 00:00:50,495
flat sheet of gas, orbiting the black hole
in concentric rings.

12
00:00:50,867 --> 00:00:55,976
As gas drifts inward, friction between
neighboring rings turns gravitational

13
00:00:55,976 --> 00:01:00,887
energy into heat, and each ring radiates
that heat away from its surface.

14
00:01:01,267 --> 00:01:06,507
Balancing the energy released at each
radius against the energy radiated gives

15
00:01:06,507 --> 00:01:08,762
the local temperature of the disk.

16
00:01:09,100 --> 00:01:14,181
Far from the black hole, the bracket is
close to one, so the temperature falls off

17
00:01:14,181 --> 00:01:18,956
like r to the minus three quarters. But
the disk ends at the innermost stable

18
00:01:18,956 --> 00:01:21,895
orbit, and there the temperature drops to
zero.

19
00:01:22,233 --> 00:01:27,531
So the hottest gas sits just outside the
inner edge, at forty-nine thirty-sixths of

20
00:01:27,531 --> 00:01:28,603
the inner radius.

21
00:01:30,400 --> 00:01:35,439
How does a disk like this look spectrally?
The key assumption is that the disk is

22
00:01:35,439 --> 00:01:40,170
optically thick, so each ring glows like a
blackbody at its own temperature.

23
00:01:40,500 --> 00:01:46,336
A hot inner ring peaks at high frequency.
A cool outer ring peaks at low frequency,

24
00:01:46,336 --> 00:01:48,420
but it has a much larger area.

25
00:01:48,767 --> 00:01:54,357
Add them all up, ring by ring, and you get
something no single blackbody can produce:

26
00:01:54,357 --> 00:01:56,437
a broad, stretched-out spectrum.

27
00:01:56,767 --> 00:02:02,233
At low frequencies every ring is in its
Rayleigh-Jeans tail, so the spectrum rises

28
00:02:02,233 --> 00:02:07,830
like nu squared. At high frequencies it is
cut off exponentially by the hottest gas.

29
00:02:07,830 --> 00:02:13,165
And in between, the overlapping rings
combine into a gentle power law, nu to the

30
00:02:13,165 --> 00:02:16,787
one third: the signature of a multi-
temperature disk.

31
00:02:18,567 --> 00:02:23,377
Here's where it gets interesting. The
temperature scale of the disk depends on

32
00:02:23,377 --> 00:02:28,309
just one combination: the accretion rate
divided by the mass squared, to the one

33
00:02:28,309 --> 00:02:29,162
quarter power.

34
00:02:29,500 --> 00:02:34,536
Compare black holes feeding at the same
fraction of their Eddington limit, and the

35
00:02:34,536 --> 00:02:39,694
accretion rate grows in proportion to the
mass. So the temperature goes like mass to

36
00:02:39,694 --> 00:02:43,395
the minus one quarter. Bigger black holes
have cooler disks.

37
00:02:43,733 --> 00:02:49,537
Watch the spectrum as we dial up the mass.
The disk becomes vastly more luminous, but

38
00:02:49,537 --> 00:02:52,978
its peak slides steadily toward lower
frequencies.

39
00:02:53,167 --> 00:02:58,600
A ten solar mass black hole peaks near one
kilo-electron-volt, in soft X-rays. A

40
00:02:58,600 --> 00:03:03,497
hundred million solar mass black hole
peaks near seven hundred and forty

41
00:03:03,497 --> 00:03:08,997
angstroms, in the far ultraviolet. The
ratio is ten million to the one quarter: a

42
00:03:08,997 --> 00:03:12,687
factor of fifty-six. Same equations,
different colors.

43
00:03:13,033 --> 00:03:18,675
Changing the accretion rate does something
different. The luminosity rises in direct

44
00:03:18,675 --> 00:03:23,985
proportion, but the peak barely moves: a
hundredfold increase shifts it by only

45
00:03:23,985 --> 00:03:25,578
about a factor of three.

46
00:03:27,367 --> 00:03:32,439
What does a telescope actually see? Tilt
the disk away from us, and its projected

47
00:03:32,439 --> 00:03:37,203
area shrinks by the cosine of the
inclination. Move it farther away, and the

48
00:03:37,203 --> 00:03:39,987
flux falls as one over the distance
squared.

49
00:03:40,333 --> 00:03:46,041
The observer sees the same spectral shape,
just scaled down. Assume the disk shines

50
00:03:46,041 --> 00:03:51,342
equally in all directions, and you
misjudge its luminosity by a factor of two

51
00:03:51,342 --> 00:03:51,953
cosine i.

52
00:03:53,733 --> 00:03:58,753
To compute all of this, we take the
temperature profile from the published

53
00:03:58,753 --> 00:04:03,438
agnpy package, use astropy's blackbody
function, and sum two thousand

54
00:04:03,438 --> 00:04:05,378
logarithmically spaced rings.

55
00:04:05,700 --> 00:04:10,805
The total luminosity matches the exact
formula to a few parts in a million, and

56
00:04:10,805 --> 00:04:14,570
the error shrinks as one over the number
of rings squared.

57
00:04:16,367 --> 00:04:21,155
The real test, of course, is nature.
First, quasars: this is the average

58
00:04:21,155 --> 00:04:26,337
spectrum of more than two thousand of
them, from the Sloan Digital Sky Survey.

59
00:04:26,667 --> 00:04:32,082
A thin disk for a typical quasar captures
the overall shape, the big blue bump

60
00:04:32,082 --> 00:04:37,839
rising into the far ultraviolet. But the
real spectrum is redder, especially toward

61
00:04:37,839 --> 00:04:38,662
the optical.

62
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:43,956
Go further, with a hundred thousand
individual quasars. For each one, predict

63
00:04:43,956 --> 00:04:49,165
the continuum slope from its own mass and
accretion rate. In the far ultraviolet,

64
00:04:49,165 --> 00:04:54,057
the model matches on average. In the
optical, almost nine out of ten quasars

65
00:04:54,057 --> 00:04:59,267
are redder than a bare disk. Something
else is in the mix: dust, the host galaxy,

66
00:04:59,267 --> 00:05:00,220
extra emission.

67
00:05:00,567 --> 00:05:06,506
Now a stellar-mass black hole: LMC X-3, in
the Large Magellanic Cloud. Its mass,

68
00:05:06,506 --> 00:05:12,299
distance and inclination are measured
independently. We fold the model through

69
00:05:12,299 --> 00:05:17,578
the X-ray detector's response and fit
forty-eight spectra from the RXTE

70
00:05:17,578 --> 00:05:18,312
satellite.

71
00:05:18,633 --> 00:05:23,689
It fits beautifully, but only if each
ring's light is hardened: it looks like a

72
00:05:23,689 --> 00:05:28,808
blackbody about one point eight times
hotter, then diluted. That is exactly what

73
00:05:28,808 --> 00:05:33,926
electron scattering in a disk atmosphere
is predicted to do. A pure blackbody is

74
00:05:33,926 --> 00:05:35,253
ruled out completely.

75
00:05:37,033 --> 00:05:42,539
So a handful of ideas, energy balance,
blackbody rings and a fourth-root scaling,

76
00:05:42,539 --> 00:05:48,045
explain why the same physics paints small
black holes in X-rays and giant ones in

77
00:05:48,045 --> 00:05:49,253
ultraviolet light.

78
00:05:49,600 --> 00:05:54,322
The model leaves things out: general
relativity, black hole spin, the hot

79
00:05:54,322 --> 00:05:59,810
corona, and the messy surroundings of real
quasars. But that is what makes it useful.

80
00:05:59,810 --> 00:06:03,320
Where it fails tells us exactly what else
is going on.

